#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# is_coprime

#% text
# `is_coprime(a, b, ...)` gives `true` when the integers are pairwise coprime, with no prime factor in common.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# With two arguments it is `gcd(a, b) == 1`; with more, every pair must be coprime.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
is_coprime(8, 15)
#% output
#= true

#% cell
is_coprime(8, 14)
#% output
#= false

#% cell
is_coprime(6, 35, 11)
#% output
#= true

#% cell
is_coprime(6, 35, 10)
#% output
#= false

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The chance that two random integers are coprime is `6/pi^2`; among the pairs below 100:

#% cell
n(count_if(tuples(range(100), 2), p => is_coprime(p[1], p[2]))/100^2)
#% output
#= 0.6087

#% cell
6/pi^2.0
#% output
#= 0.6079271018540267

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Pairwise coprime is stronger than a gcd of 1:

#% cell
[is_coprime(6, 10, 15), gcd(6, 10, 15)]
#% output
#= [false, 1]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `gcd`, `divisible`, `euler_phi`
